  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Charge on capacitor after charging with a battery of potential <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></math></p><p>Now, capacitor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> is connected as shown in the figure.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0130_0286b076880d.png\" style=\"width: 251px; height: 184px;\" /></p><p>As diode is in reverse bias, so capacitor charge remains same after time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>R</mi></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></math></p><p>In case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math>, capacitor is connected as shown in the figure.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0130_6d5efe47548f.png\" style=\"width: 249px; height: 177px;\" /></p><p>Here, diode is in forward bias, so capacitor discharges and its charge decays as&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#183;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></math></p><p>where,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>C</mi></math></p>"
  }
}